static const char REMOTE_MANIFEST_JSON[] = "{\"builtin\":true,\"caps\":{\"ask\":{\"args\":{\"options\":{\"doc\":\"The choices; blank: Yes and No.\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"str\"},\"type\":\"list\"},\"prompt\":{\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"},\"timeout\":{\"default\":60,\"doc\":\"Seconds to wait for an answer.\",\"max\":600,\"min\":5,\"type\":\"int\"}},\"doc\":\"Ask the person holding the remote a question and wait for their answer. The screen shows the prompt and the options; they step through options with the navigation buttons and pick one with OK. Answers {choice, index, button}, or {timeout true} when nobody answered in time.\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"write\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"bt\":{\"args\":{\"action\":{\"default\":\"status\",\"enum\":[\"status\",\"pair\",\"forget\",\"find\",\"quiet\"],\"type\":\"str\"},\"address\":{\"doc\":\"For pair: the remote to bond with (aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff, as heard lists it); left out, the first one heard in pairing mode.\",\"max\":17,\"type\":\"str\"},\"level\":{\"default\":3,\"max\":3,\"min\":1,\"type\":\"int\"},\"names\":{\"doc\":\"What its keys are called, raw key name (bt_c3) to the name it is sent under (up). Merged into the names kept: each name given adds or replaces that key's, a blank or null one takes that key's name out; keys not given keep theirs.\",\"type\":\"map\"}},\"doc\":\"The Bluetooth remote paired to this one (most Android TV remotes). Its keys arrive as remote.button events too, with source bt; a key that means what one of this remote's buttons means has that button's name, any other is bt_c<usage> or bt_k<code>. The gear key (HID Menu 0x40, 0x9F as the onn remote sends it, or AL Control Panel 0x19F) is settings. In the remote's menu it is the options key; elsewhere it goes to the device picked as its menu key. A remote whose gear key arrives as something else gets it by naming that raw key settings in names. action pair looks for a remote in pairing mode for 30 seconds and bonds with the first one heard, or with the one at address (an address from heard); forget drops it; blank answers its state (off, idle, pairing, waiting, connected), name, address, last raw report, why the last pair did not bond (pair_error, one of none found, not heard, no answer, refused, not a remote), while the remote's own Settings > Pair BT list is open the HID remotes it hears (heard, each with address, name, rssi, and pairing when it says it is in pairing mode), its GATT services and whether it has a microphone the remote can use (voice). find makes a paired remote with a buzzer beep so it can be found (level 1 to 3, quiet to loud; Google's Find My Remote service), quiet stops it; find in the answer says whether it has the service. On the remote, Settings > Pair BT lists the HID remotes heard advertising, with their names and signal, as they turn up; OK on one bonds with it, back leaves it. A bridge with no remote bonded keeps looking by itself and bonds with the first HID remote that says it is in pairing mode (never while one is bonded).\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"write\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"chat\":{\"args\":{\"clear\":{\"default\":false,\"type\":\"bool\"},\"status\":{\"type\":\"str\"},\"text\":{\"type\":\"str\"},\"who\":{\"default\":\"agent\",\"enum\":[\"you\",\"agent\",\"note\"],\"type\":\"str\"}},\"doc\":\"The conversation with the house agent on the remote's screen: the last dozen things said, in bubbles (the person's on the right, the agent's on the left, notes bare), scrolled with up and down; it is the remote's menu's Assistant tab, where OK opens the paired remote's microphone like its Assistant key. text adds one, said by who (you: the person, agent, or note), and brings the conversation up; status sets the line under it (Thinking...; blank: none); clear empties it. Answers the conversation as it stands: {messages: [{who, text}]}.\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"write\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"device\":{\"args\":{\"action\":{\"default\":\"key\",\"enum\":[\"key\",\"launch\",\"power\",\"pair\",\"forget\",\"select\",\"release\"],\"type\":\"str\"},\"app\":{\"type\":\"str\"},\"device\":{\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"},\"key\":{\"type\":\"str\"}},\"doc\":\"Act on a device the scan found, straight from the remote over the local network, no hub in between. device is its id, name or address. action key presses key (up, down, left, right, ok, back, home, menu, vol_up, vol_down, mute, play, pause, rewind, forward, power, or the device's own key name in its own spelling); launch starts app (youtube, netflix, prime, disney, hulu, appletv, plex, or the device's own app id; a Roku also takes an app's name); power turns it off, or wakes it when it is off; pair has an LG TV show its prompt and waits a minute for someone to accept (a Roku needs none); forget drops the pairing; select makes the remote's own buttons drive this device from now on, and release hands them back to the hub's target.\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"write\",\"scope\":\"external\"},\"devices\":{\"doc\":\"What the last scan found, without scanning again, and the device the remote's buttons are driving now (selected), if any; with the IR profiles the remote keeps (profiles; remote.ir) and the one its buttons are for (selected_profile).\",\"idempotent\":true,\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"read\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"display\":{\"args\":{\"seconds\":{\"default\":0,\"max\":86400,\"min\":0,\"type\":\"int\"},\"text\":{\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"}},\"doc\":\"Show text on the remote's screen (held upright, 16 lines of 5 characters; newlines split lines, long lines wrap). seconds > 0 goes back to the status screen after that long; 0 keeps it until replaced.\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"write\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"ir\":{\"args\":{\"action\":{\"default\":\"status\",\"enum\":[\"status\",\"write\",\"program\",\"suppress\",\"clear\",\"pin\",\"send\",\"profiles\",\"profile_save\",\"profile_delete\",\"profile_select\",\"profile_release\",\"profile_send\"],\"type\":\"str\"},\"address\":{\"doc\":\"The NEC address (over 255: extended).\",\"max\":65535,\"min\":0,\"type\":\"int\"},\"carrier\":{\"default\":38000,\"doc\":\"The carrier in Hz, for raw.\",\"max\":100000,\"min\":20000,\"type\":\"int\"},\"char\":{\"doc\":\"c1..c5, for write.\",\"type\":\"str\"},\"code\":{\"doc\":\"Bytes in hex (spaces and colons are ignored).\",\"max\":2048,\"type\":\"str\"},\"command\":{\"doc\":\"The NEC command.\",\"max\":255,\"min\":0,\"type\":\"int\"},\"icon\":{\"doc\":\"For profile_save: the profile's picture, tv, projector, speaker, streaming, light, phone, car, game or generic (blank: none, shown as generic; left out: as it was).\",\"max\":20,\"type\":\"str\"},\"key\":{\"doc\":\"An Android keycode (0..65535), for write and program; a key's name, for profile_send.\",\"type\":\"json\"},\"keys\":{\"doc\":\"Android keycodes (a list), for suppress; for profile_save, a map of key name to {protocol: nec, address, command}, {raw: [...], carrier}, {lan: true | key}, {cap, worker, args} or null.\",\"type\":\"json\"},\"link\":{\"doc\":\"For profile_save: the network device that keys with no action (and lan actions) go to (blank: none).\",\"max\":40,\"type\":\"str\"},\"merge\":{\"default\":true,\"doc\":\"For profile_save: false replaces the whole profile.\",\"type\":\"bool\"},\"name\":{\"doc\":\"A profile's name, for the profile actions.\",\"max\":20,\"type\":\"str\"},\"pin\":{\"doc\":\"The IR LED's GPIO, for pin (-1: none).\",\"max\":48,\"min\":-1,\"type\":\"int\"},\"protocol\":{\"doc\":\"For send.\",\"enum\":[\"nec\"],\"type\":\"str\"},\"raw\":{\"doc\":\"Microsecond durations, mark first, for send.\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"int\"},\"max\":1024,\"type\":\"list\"},\"repeats\":{\"default\":0,\"doc\":\"NEC repeat frames after the first, for send and profile_send.\",\"max\":4,\"min\":0,\"type\":\"int\"}},\"doc\":\"Raw access to the paired remote's IR-over-BLE service (D343BFC0-5A21-4F05-BC7D-AF01F617B664, on the onn remote), to find its IR code format by experiment. Characteristics by suffix: c1 IR_PROG_CONTROL (01 start a session, 00 finish), c2 IR_KEY_ID (an Android keycode, 2 bytes little-endian), c3 IR_CODE (the code, split over writes), c4 IR_SUPPRESS (keycodes, 2 bytes each, that stay BLE), c5 IR_KEY_EVENT (notifies 00 down / 01 up, then the keycode). action write writes code (hex) to char, chunked at the MTU (code blank and key given: the key's 2 bytes); program does start, key, code in 20-byte writes, finish; suppress writes keys to c4; clear writes an empty suppress list and forgets the key events seen; blank only reports. Every write is with response: writes lists one line per write, '<char> <hex>: ok', or 'att 0x..' (the remote refused) or 'err N'. events lists the IR_KEY_EVENT notifications of the last 5 s (ago_ms, down or up, key, raw hex); those keys also arrive as remote.button ir_<keycode hex> when no HID key came with them. Android keycodes: POWER 26, VOLUME_UP 24, VOLUME_DOWN 25, MUTE 164, TV_INPUT 178. The remote can also send IR itself, from an IR LED on one of its own GPIOs (pin HIGH = LED on; the pin idles low): action pin sets that GPIO (-1: none; kept across restarts; the screen's pins are refused, a strapping pin is set with a warning) and answers it. action send sends, with the hardware carrier, either protocol nec with address (0..255, or a 16-bit extended address above 255), command (0..255) and repeats (repeat frames 108 ms apart), or raw: microsecond durations, mark first, alternately mark and space, at carrier Hz. A send is refused when it would last over 500 ms or has a mark over 20 ms, and is an error when no pin is set. It answers what was sent (sent), pin, duration_us, carrier_hz and edges. Every answer has ir_pin, the pin set (-1: none). Profiles are the devices the remote keeps itself, and the bridge is their dispatcher: every key of the paired remote is looked up in the profile picked and does that key's action, with no hub and no Wi-Fi needed for ir. A profile has a name, an optional link to a device on its network (remote.devices, by id, name or address), and keys, an action for each of the keys power, vol_up, vol_down, mute, input, up, down, left, right, ok, back, home, menu (also called settings), play, pause, rewind, forward, ch_up, ch_down, info, exit, stop, next, prev and 0..9. An action is ir, a code: {protocol: nec, address, command} or {raw: [durations], carrier}; lan, {lan: true} (the key itself) or {lan: \\\"<key>\\\"} (another key, in the device's own spelling too) sent to the linked device over the network; or cap, {cap: \\\"plugin.verb\\\", worker: \\\"<worker>\\\", args: {...}}, a Rook cap call the remote cannot make itself (a worker cannot start a call): the key's remote.button event carries it as call {cap, worker, args} with via cap and profile, for the hub to make. A key with no action goes to the linked device as lan when there is one. Only kept profiles are profiles: a device a scan found is not one until a profile is made for it (profile_save with link, or OK on it in the remote's Settings > Find devs); what scans found is remote.devices. The remote's Devices list and its profile-key round are the kept profiles and nothing else, picked from there; the choice is kept across restarts. On the remote, Add device in the Devices list names a new empty profile (on-screen keyboard) and picks it; Settings > Find devs lists what the scan found (* on those with a profile; Rescan scans again), and OK on one picks its profile, or makes one named after it, linked to it (keys with no action go to it, lan; a device the remote has no driver for gets an IR-only profile the same way) and picks it. The options key on a profile in Devices offers Rename, Icon (a picture drawn by its name in Devices and on the status screen while it is picked: tv, projector, speaker, streaming, light, phone, car, game or generic; none shows generic) and Delete (asked to confirm). Keys are set with profile_save. Held on the paired remote, an NEC code is followed by repeat frames every 108 ms, a raw one sent again, for volume, arrows, channels and seek only (power never repeats, and nothing goes on past 30 s). action profiles lists them (name, link, link_name, icon, keys with an action, lan_keys and cap_keys: which of them are lan and cap, bytes) with selected, max, bytes, bytes_max and icons (the icon names). profile_save makes or changes profile name: link (blank: none; left out: as it was), icon (an icon name; blank: none; left out: as it was), keys a map of key to an action as above, or null to take a key's action out; merge (true) adds and replaces just the keys given, false makes the profile exactly these. Raw codes are checked against the send limits when saved; cap args are at most 240 bytes as JSON. A request is at most 16384 bytes with its ticket (a bigger one is answered args too large). At most 8 profiles, 256 durations a raw code, 1536 bytes a profile and 4096 in all (an NEC code takes 5). profile_delete drops name for good, with the pairing the remote holds with its linked device (an LG key) unless another profile is linked to it, and lets the buttons go if they were its; profile_select makes the remote's buttons drive name; profile_release hands them back to the hub's target; profile_send does key of name (blank: the selected one) now: an ir code is sent, answering as send does; lan goes to the device (sent says so); a cap action is not made but answered as call.\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"write\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"map\":{\"args\":{\"names\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"str\"},\"type\":\"list\"},\"screen_off\":{\"doc\":\"Seconds left alone before the screen goes dark (30; 0: never).\",\"max\":86400,\"min\":0,\"type\":\"int\"},\"sleep\":{\"doc\":\"Seconds left alone before the remote sleeps: Wi-Fi off, so the hub cannot reach it until a key wakes it (300; 0: never).\",\"max\":86400,\"min\":0,\"type\":\"int\"},\"targets\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"str\"},\"type\":\"list\"},\"tz\":{\"doc\":\"The remote's time zone for its clock, as POSIX TZ (CST6CDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0 for US Central); blank: UTC.\",\"max\":64,\"type\":\"str\"}},\"doc\":\"Name the buttons and the devices, and say how soon the remote rests. names lists one name per button, in the order remote.state lists them; blank keeps the current names. targets lists the hub's names for the devices its keys are for; the one picked (the one picked before, else the first) rides every remote.button event as target while no profile (remote.ir) is picked. The remote's own menu no longer lists them, as its Devices list is the profiles kept on the remote.\",\"effects\":[\"persists\"],\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"write\",\"scope\":\"self\"},\"press\":{\"args\":{\"button\":{\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"}},\"doc\":\"Press a key as if on the Bluetooth remote paired to this one (up, down, left, right, ok, back, home, input, vol_up...): it does whatever that key does now, in the menu, the game or the conversation, or goes to the device the remote drives. Wakes the remote.\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"write\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"scan\":{\"doc\":\"Look for devices on the Wi-Fi network the remote has joined (SSDP and mDNS, about 15 seconds) and answer what it found. Each device has an id (its MAC address when known), name, model, address, the kinds it answered as (roku, webos, cast, androidtv, airplay, dial, samsung, wled...), the driver the remote has for it (roku or webos; blank when it can only be seen), what that driver supports, and whether it is paired. The remote also scans on its own a few seconds after joining a network.\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"read\",\"scope\":\"external\"},\"screen\":{\"doc\":\"The screen's picture right now: {width, height, rotation (quarter turns clockwise it is shown at), dark (the panel is off), says (the same in words), data}. data is base64 of the panel's own buffer before rotation: one byte for each 8 pixels down a column (lowest bit on top), columns left to right, in bands of 8 rows.\",\"idempotent\":true,\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"read\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"state\":{\"doc\":\"The remote's buttons (names, which are held), the last presses, the device its buttons are for (target, of targets), its mode (remote, menu, chat or game) and the menu tab open (tab, of Devices, Assistant, Games, Settings), what the screen shows, Wi-Fi and hub status, the device on its own network it is driving (local), the IR profile its buttons are for (profile) and how many profiles it keeps (profiles), free memory, the firmware update's state, and the firmware's version and build (a three-word moniker derived from its sources, the same for the same sources).\",\"idempotent\":true,\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"read\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"update\":{\"args\":{\"sha256\":{\"type\":\"str\"},\"url\":{\"type\":\"str\"}},\"doc\":\"Update the remote's firmware over the air. url is where the build is (http or https, reachable from the network the remote is on) and sha256 its SHA-256 in hex; the remote downloads it into its spare slot, checks the hash, answers started and restarts into it. A download that stalls (20 s with no byte) or breaks off is tried again, up to 5 times in all, from where it got to (an HTTP Range request), with Bluetooth scanning paused meanwhile. The new build is kept once it has settled (on Wi-Fi, and on a bridge with a bonded remote, that remote connected again) or reached the hub, whichever comes first; with neither in five minutes the remote goes back to the build before. Settings > Update on the remote reads the feed http://rook.bakeforge.com/firmware/latest-<board>.json ({version, moniker, url, sha256}) itself and installs the build when it is newer, and asks the hub (remote.update.check) only when the feed cannot be read. Blank answers the running version and build (its three-word moniker), the update's state (idle, checking, newest, downloading, failed, restarting), whether the build is still on trial, and after a download tries, heap_min (the least free heap while it ran), and when it failed failed_heap and failed_block (free heap and the largest piece of it then).\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"exec\",\"scope\":\"self\"},\"wait\":{\"args\":{\"buttons\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"str\"},\"type\":\"list\"},\"timeout\":{\"default\":30,\"doc\":\"Seconds to wait.\",\"max\":600,\"min\":1,\"type\":\"int\"}},\"doc\":\"Wait for the next button press on the remote. Answers {button, action, held_ms}, or {timeout true}. buttons narrows it to those button names.\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"read\",\"scope\":\"local\"},\"wifi\":{\"args\":{\"action\":{\"default\":\"list\",\"enum\":[\"list\",\"add\",\"forget\"],\"type\":\"str\"},\"password\":{\"type\":\"str\"},\"prefer\":{\"doc\":\"A saved network's name to move to the top of the list (joined first from now on).\",\"type\":\"str\"},\"ssid\":{\"type\":\"str\"}},\"doc\":\"The Wi-Fi networks the remote knows, in the order it joins them: on starting up or losing its network it scans once and tries each saved network it hears, first to last, moving on when one fails and going round again when all have. Names match whatever their case. Blank lists their names (saved, in that order) and the one in use. action add saves ssid with password at the top of the list; forget drops ssid. prefer moves a saved network (by name) to the top and keeps it there; it is an error when no saved network has that name. A person can do the same on the remote, in its Wi-Fi menu (saved networks first, in this order, then the others heard, each with its signal in bars): OK on a network joins it at once, with its stored password when it is saved (the on-screen keyboard asks only for a new network, or when the network refuses the stored password); the paired remote's settings key on a network offers Join, and on a saved one Move up, Move down (this order) and Forget.\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"map\"},\"risk\":\"write\",\"scope\":\"self\"}},\"doc\":\"A handheld button remote with a small screen. Button presses arrive as remote.button events; agents can show text on it, ask a question and get the answer from a button press, or wait for the next press.\",\"emits\":{\"remote.button\":{\"doc\":\"A button on the remote was pressed (action press) or held down (action long, once while held). Not sent for the key that opens the remote's menu (input on the handheld; Guide on the bridge, where Guide held opens the conversation and input goes to the TV), nor the paired remote's profile key (it steps to the next device), nor while the menu or the game is up (in the menu the paired remote's settings key is the options key, with Rename or Delete on a profile in Devices, Join, Move up, Move down or Forget on a network).\",\"payload\":{\"action\":{\"doc\":\"press or long.\",\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"},\"button\":{\"doc\":\"The button's name.\",\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"},\"call\":{\"doc\":\"With via cap: the profile's cap action for this key, {cap, worker, args}, for the hub to make (a worker cannot start a call).\",\"type\":\"map\"},\"held_ms\":{\"type\":\"int\"},\"index\":{\"doc\":\"The button's position in remote.state.\",\"type\":\"int\"},\"local\":{\"doc\":\"true when target is a profile or a device on the remote's own network that the remote dispatched the key to itself (remote.ir profiles, remote.device select).\",\"type\":\"bool\"},\"profile\":{\"doc\":\"The profile picked, when the key went through one.\",\"type\":\"str\"},\"source\":{\"doc\":\"bt when the key was pressed on the Bluetooth remote paired to this one.\",\"type\":\"str\"},\"target\":{\"doc\":\"The device picked in the remote's menu (an appliance's name).\",\"type\":\"str\"},\"via\":{\"doc\":\"What the key did: ir (a code sent), lan (a key sent to a network device), cap (call carries the profile's cap action; the remote did nothing itself).\",\"type\":\"str\"}}},\"remote.update.check\":{\"doc\":\"Someone picked Update in the remote's menu and the remote could not read the update feed itself (feed_error says why). The hub's remote-update plugin answers over the band, with remote.update when it holds a newer build for this board.\",\"payload\":{\"board\":{\"doc\":\"bridge-c3, s3 or gamepad-s3: which build it takes.\",\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"},\"build\":{\"doc\":\"The build's three-word moniker.\",\"type\":\"str\"},\"feed_error\":{\"doc\":\"Why the update feed could not be read.\",\"type\":\"str\"},\"version\":{\"doc\":\"The firmware it runs.\",\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"},\"worker\":{\"doc\":\"This remote's name.\",\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"}}},\"remote.voice\":{\"doc\":\"Someone finished saying something into the microphone of the Bluetooth remote paired to this one (its Assistant key). The audio came before it, in parts remote.voice.part events with the same id.\",\"payload\":{\"bytes\":{\"type\":\"int\"},\"codec\":{\"doc\":\"adpcm8k or adpcm16k: Google's voice-over-BLE frames (ATVV) of IMA ADPCM, mono.\",\"type\":\"str\"},\"id\":{\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"},\"ms\":{\"doc\":\"How long the microphone was open.\",\"type\":\"int\"},\"parts\":{\"doc\":\"How many remote.voice.part events carried it.\",\"required\":true,\"type\":\"int\"},\"worker\":{\"doc\":\"This remote's name.\",\"type\":\"str\"}}},\"remote.voice.part\":{\"doc\":\"A piece of what is being said (see remote.voice).\",\"payload\":{\"data\":{\"doc\":\"Base64 of the audio bytes the remote sent, in order.\",\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"},\"id\":{\"required\":true,\"type\":\"str\"},\"seq\":{\"required\":true,\"type\":\"int\"}}}},\"license\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"manifest\":1,\"package\":\"core\",\"plugin\":\"remote\",\"source\":\"https://github.com/Bake-Ware/rook\",\"title\":\"Rook remote\",\"version\":\"0.12.2\"}";
